4. Top blockers

1. The plan’s own review gate is failed, not passed. Both saved review artifacts for this exact plan are still `MAJOR`, but the plan rewrites that history as `MINOR` and “approval-ready.”
2. The downstream #2247 handoff contract is described inaccurately. The current writeback implementation in [phase-c-classify.py](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/scripts/data/document-index/phase-c-classify.py) is content-hash allowlist driven, not a generic manifest-rule consumer.
3. The scoring rubric relies on fields and semantics that were not retrieved from authoritative implementation surfaces. In particular, `readability` is not established as an available index/writeback field in the consulted evidence.
4. Retrieval did not meet the repo’s data-pipeline planning contract because the plan skipped key machine-readable implementation surfaces such as `scripts/data/document-index/config.yaml` and the actual writeback/classification scripts before claiming approval readiness.

5. Critical findings

- The plan falsifies adversarial review state. [scripts/review/results/2026-04-13-plan-2249-hermes.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/scripts/review/results/2026-04-13-plan-2249-hermes.md) says `Overall verdict: MAJOR`; [scripts/review/results/2026-04-13-plan-2249-subagent.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/scripts/review/results/2026-04-13-plan-2249-subagent.md) says `Overall verdict: MAJOR` and `Ready for user approval: No`. The plan file claims `Overall result: MINOR (approval-ready with implementation-time caution)`. That is a workflow/governance violation under [issue-planning-mode](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/.claude/skills/coordination/issue-planning-mode/SKILL.md), which requires revision and re-review if any reviewer returns `MAJOR`.
- The #2247 contract is overstated and partly invented. The plan says #2247 “consumes the handoff manifest” and supports records “explicitly listed or matched by manifest rule,” but [phase-c-classify.py](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/scripts/data/document-index/phase-c-classify.py) currently exposes bounded writeback via `--target-list` of `content_hash` values. That is materially narrower than the proposed manifest semantics.
- The rubric is not grounded in retrieved schema. The plan’s pseudocode uses `readability in {native,ocr,missing}`, but the retrieved implementation surfaces show `content_hash`, `source`, `ext`, `path_category`, `path_subcategory`, `domain`, `status`, and summary/extraction fields such as `extraction_method` and `discipline` instead. The plan introduces a decision axis it did not verify exists.
- The issue is labeled `cat:data-pipeline`, so [HARD-STOP-POLICY](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/docs/standards/HARD-STOP-POLICY.md) applies. Given the unresolved `MAJOR` reviews, this is not approval-ready regardless of local plan wording or stale labels.

6. High findings

- Retrieval missed required data-pipeline implementation surfaces. [docs/plans/README.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/docs/plans/README.md) says data-pipeline issues should consult `registry.yaml`, pipeline config, and `resource-intelligence-maturity.yaml`. The plan cited `registry.yaml` and maturity YAML, but not the pipeline config and not the actual classifier/writeback scripts before defining execution and handoff behavior.
- The plan claims “source-root-first” decomposition without reconciling source-root registry semantics against current index reality. [data-audit-report.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/data/document-index/data-audit-report.md) reports 6 distinct sources, while [mounted-source-registry.yaml](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/data/document-index/mounted-source-registry.yaml) contains a much broader source-root catalog. The plan does not define how indexed `source` values map to registry roots for packing.
- The proposed output may generate writeback targets outside the current authoritative taxonomy. [phase-c-classify.py](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/scripts/data/document-index/phase-c-classify.py) has a constrained `VALID_DOMAINS` list. The plan asks for “recommended classification tags or domain targets” but does not constrain recommendations to domains that the current writeback path can actually persist.
- The plan says there are no open questions left for approval readiness. That is not credible while schema assumptions, source-root mapping, and #2247 consumer semantics remain unresolved.

7. Medium findings

- The TDD list is pointed at the wrong authority for the baseline count. `test_other_bucket_count_matches_authoritative_audit` hard-codes the audit count instead of asserting live-index count and reconciling it against `registry.yaml` and `data-audit-report.md`.
- The plan cites `index.jsonl` as authoritative but does not show retrieval from it beyond assertion. It should prove the live slice shape and count from machine-readable inspection, not merely mention the file path.
- The plan says `enhancement-plan.yaml` is “older” and non-authoritative, which may be reasonable, but it does not define a concrete exclusion rule for any fields/examples borrowed from that artifact.
- “Exact selection rules” and “exact allowlists” are both mentioned, but the plan does not decide which one is authoritative when they disagree. That matters because the current #2247 path is allowlist-based.

8. Low findings

- Listing “Update GitHub issue #2249” under “Files to Change” is sloppy plan-stage phrasing; issue comments/labels are workflow actions, not repo files.
- The plan README row marks #2249 as `adversarial-reviewed`, but the actual saved reviews still fail approval readiness.
- `resource-intelligence-maturity.yaml` is used more as narrative support than as a direct execution input.

9. Required revisions before user approval

- Revise the plan to reflect the real review state: `MAJOR`, not `MINOR`.
- Re-run adversarial review after revision and do not surface for approval until all plan-stage blockers are resolved.
